# =====================================================================
#  PART ONE: THE INGENIOUS GENTLEMAN - English text
#  Locations, objects and long messages (same names as parte1_mundo.paw)
# =====================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the house
/LOC biblioteca
You are in your library. There are books of chivalry everywhere: on
the shelves, on the chairs, on the floor and, above all, in your head.
You sold many acres of good arable land to buy them. The only door
leads east, to the hall.

/LOC sala
You are in the hall of your house, the house of a gentleman of the
sort that keeps a lance in the rack, an old buckler, and a pot with
rather more beef than mutton in it. To the west is the library, to the
north your bedchamber, to the east the kitchen and to the south the
entrance. A narrow stair goes up to the attic.

/LOC aposento
Your bedchamber: a narrow bed, a pine chest at its foot and a barred
window giving onto the street. The door leads south, to the hall.

/LOC cocina
The kitchen. A pot boils on the fire. On the wall there is a cupboard
where the housekeeper keeps the food and, while she is about it, the
keys of the whole house. The hall lies to the west.

/LOC desvan
The attic, full of dust and junk. In a corner, forgotten, lie the arms
of your great-grandfathers. A stair goes down to the hall.

/LOC zaguan
The entrance. The front door opens onto the street, to the south. To
the east is the yard, and to the north the hall.

/LOC corral
The yard of your house. Rocinante lives here, if you can call that
living. To the east there is a back door that opens onto the fields.
The entrance lies to the west.

/LOC calle
The street of your village, asleep. Above you is the window of your
bedchamber. The house is locked up tight. The road leaves the village
to the east.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- first sally
/LOC montiel1
The ancient and famous plain of Montiel. The sun beats down so fiercely
that it would melt your brains, if you had any left. The back door of
your yard lies to the west and the road goes on to the east.

/LOC montiel2
You ride almost all day without anything happening worth the telling,
which drives you to despair. The road goes on to the east. In the
distance, as evening falls, an inn can be seen.

/LOC venta_puerta
Before you rises an inn. That is to say: a castle with its four towers
and pinnacles of shining silver, with its drawbridge and deep moat.
That is what you see. The door of the inn is to the east and, through
a gap in the wall, you can reach the yard to the north. The road goes
on to the south.

/LOC venta_sala
The inside of the inn, which you call a castle. A few tables, a bench,
a smell of salt cod and cheap wine. The way out is to the west.

/LOC venta_patio
The inn yard. Next to a well there is a trough where the carriers
water their mules. It is the perfect place to keep vigil over your
arms, since the chapel, according to the castellan, has been pulled
down to be built anew. The way out is to the south.

/LOC lindero
The edge of a wood. The road from the inn lies to the north. From the
thicket, to the east, come faint cries, as of someone in distress.

/LOC encinar_andres
A clearing in the wood with a great oak in the middle. The road goes on
to the east and back to the west.

/LOC encrucijada
You come to a place where the road divides in four. You remember the
crossroads where knights errant used to stop and consider which of the
roads they would take.

/LOC mercaderes
A broad road along which comes a crowd of people: six Toledan merchants
on their way to buy silk in Murcia, with their sunshades, four servants
on horseback and three mule boys on foot.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- second sally
/LOC camino_noche
You leave the village without a word of farewell, you to your niece and
housekeeper, Sancho to his wife and children. Sancho rides his ass like
a patriarch, with his saddlebags and his wineskin. The road goes on to
the east.

/LOC campo_alba
Dawn on the plain of Montiel. Sancho asks you, for the third time,
about the island. To the east, some enormous shapes stand out on the
horizon.

/LOC molinos
You are in the middle of the plain, facing thirty or forty windmills.
Or thirty or forty monstrous giants, depending on whom you ask. A road
goes down to the south.

/LOC encinar
A grove of oaks where you spend the night. Sancho sleeps like a log
after several visits to the wineskin. You do not close your eyes,
thinking of Dulcinea, as your books require. To the east runs the road
to Puerto Lápice.

/LOC puerto_lapice
The road to Puerto Lápice, which by your reckoning is a much travelled
place, full of many and varied adventures. The road goes on to the
east.

/LOC alcana
Toledo. The Alcaná market. You are now the second author of this
history (don't ask) and you are desperately looking for the rest of
the manuscript. A boy has come to sell some old notebooks and papers
to a silk merchant. In the shade, a Morisco who reads Arabic is dozing.

/LOC majada
A goatherds' camp. Round the fire, six of them are stewing salt goat
meat in a cauldron. There is a lot of rosemary growing here. The road
goes back to the west and on to the east.

/LOC prado
A meadow full of fresh grass, beside which runs a gentle, cool stream.
It is the hour of the siesta. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC venta2_puerta
An inn in the middle of the road. You say it is a castle; Sancho says
it is an inn; the innkeeper, Juan Palomeque the Left-handed, says you
will talk about the bill later. The door is to the north. The road goes
on to the east.

/LOC venta2_cocina
The kitchen of the inn, with its fire and its smell of frying. A stair
goes up to a garret. To the east is the yard and the way out is to the
south.

/LOC camaranchon
A garret that, by the smell of it, has served as a hayloft for many
years. There are three beds: yours, four badly planed boards on two
uneven benches; a carrier's; and Sancho's, which is a mat.

/LOC corral2
The yard of the inn. A large blanket is hanging out to dry, the kind
that is used for... better not think about it. The walls are not very
high. The kitchen lies to the west.

/LOC llanura
A broad, dusty plain. The inn lies to the west. The road goes on to
the east.

/LOC camino_noche2
Night falls on you in the middle of the road, with no idea where to
find shelter. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC valle_batanes
A dark valley among tall trees. You can hear a great noise of water
and, in time with it, some fearful blows, with a clanking of iron and
chains. The night is so black that nothing can be seen. The din comes
from the east.

/LOC batanes
A meadow at the foot of some rocks, where a stream drives six fulling
hammers that pound a length of cloth with alternating blows. The road
goes on to the east.

/LOC camino_lluvia
A highroad. It is beginning to drizzle. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC camino_real
The highroad, which to the south plunges into the wilds of the Sierra
Morena.

/LOC sierra1
The wilds of the Sierra Morena. Crags, rockroses and not a soul. The
Holy Brotherhood will not find you here. Nor will anybody else, for
that matter. The way goes on to the south.

/LOC sierra2
A valley hidden among the crags of the sierra. To the east a stream
runs down.

/LOC pena_pobre
At the foot of a high mountain runs a gentle stream, and the meadow is
so green and lush that it gladdens the eyes. There are many trees. It
is the perfect place to go mad. Well, madder.

/LOC jaula
You are travelling, shut in a cage of wooden bars on an ox cart, on the
way to your village. In front go the curate and the barber, in disguise
(very bad disguise). The oxen go slowly. Very slowly.

