# =====================================================================
#  LONG MESSAGES
# =====================================================================
/MSG titulo
{i6}{b1}   DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA{b0}{i7}{n}{n}{i5}       (c) AVENTURAS LEVEL 10{i7}{n}{n}
Program and graphics:{n}{i6}Jorge Louzao Penalva{i7}{n}With the help of:{n}Angel Louzao Penalva{n}
Luis Gabaldón Alvarez{n}{i4}   1989 version - 2026 remake{i7}

/MSG prologo
In a village of La Mancha, the name of which I have no desire to call
to mind, there lived not long since one of those gentlemen that keep a
lance in the lance-rack, an old buckler, a lean hack, and a greyhound
for coursing...

Our gentleman was nearly fifty. In his idle moments, which were most of
the year, he gave himself up to reading books of chivalry with such
ardour and delight that he almost entirely neglected the management of
his estate.

/MSG ayuda_inicial
{i5}Type commands such as GET LANCE, EXAMINE ROCINANTE, N, S, E, W,
UP, DOWN or SAY TO SANCHO "HELLO". Other useful ones: I (inventory),
LOOK, EXITS, HELP, SAVE, LOAD, SCORE.{i7}

/MSG leer_libro
You open a book by Feliciano de Silva and read: {i6}"The reason of the
unreason with which my reason is afflicted so weakens my reason that
with reason I murmur at your beauty."{i7}

You read from dusk till dawn and from dawn till dusk until, from little
sleep and much reading, your brain dries up and you lose your wits. You
decide to become a knight errant and roam the world with your arms and
your horse in search of adventures.

/MSG nombre1
Four days go by while you think what name to give your hack, who has
more cracks in his hoofs than a real has quarters, and more blemishes
than the steed of Gonela. After many names that you devise, erase and
reject, you call him {i6}ROCINANTE{i7}: a name lofty, sonorous and
significant.

/MSG nombre2
It takes you eight more days to name yourself. In the end you call
yourself {i6}DON QUIXOTE{i7} and, as the valiant Amadis added the name
of his kingdom, you add the name of your country: {i6}DON QUIXOTE OF LA
MANCHA{i7}.

/MSG nombre3
A knight errant without a lady is a tree without leaves or fruit. In a
village near your own there is a very good-looking farm girl, Aldonza
Lorenzo, with whom you were in love for a time (though she never knew
it or gave it a thought). You call her {i6}DULCINEA DEL TOBOSO{i7}.

/MSG primera_salida
One morning before dawn, on one of the hottest days of July, you arm
yourself with all your armour, mount Rocinante, brace your buckler,
take up your lance and ride out through the back door of the yard into
the open country, in the greatest contentment and delight.

/MSG pensamiento_terrible
Scarcely are you out in the fields when a terrible thought strikes you:
you have not been dubbed a knight! By the law of chivalry you neither
can nor ought to bear arms against any knight. You resolve to have
yourself dubbed by the first person you meet.

/MSG llegada_venta
At the door stand two young women, of the sort they call ladies of the
road. Just then a swineherd blows a horn to gather his pigs, and you
take him for a dwarf announcing your arrival. The girls, seeing you in
armour, take fright. {i4}"Fly not, your ladyships, nor fear any
discourtesy,"{i7} you tell them. Hearing themselves called ladies, they
cannot keep from laughing. Out comes the innkeeper, a man so fat that
he is very peaceable.

/MSG ventero_armar
{i6}"Your worship has done well to come to this castle to ask that
boon. But the chapel has been pulled down to be built anew, so the arms
may be watched in the yard, beside the trough. Tomorrow, God willing,
the due ceremonies will be performed."{i7}

Then he asks whether you have any money. You tell him you have not a
farthing, for you have never read that knights errant carried any.
{i6}"Your worship is mistaken: though the histories do not write it
down, they carried well-lined purses, and clean shirts, and a little
box of ointments to cure their wounds. And a squire."{i7}

/MSG velar
You brace your buckler, grasp your lance and, with a noble bearing,
begin to pace up and down in front of the trough, where you have laid
your arms. The moon shines so brightly that everything you do is seen
by everyone. The people of the inn come out to watch.

/MSG arriero1_llega
A carrier comes to the trough to water his mules and, to do so, moves
your arms aside. {i4}"O thou, whoever thou art, rash knight, look to
what thou doest and touch them not!"{i7}

/MSG arriero1_golpe
You raise your lance in both hands and deal the carrier such a blow on
the head that you lay him on the ground, so battered that, had a second
followed, he would have needed no surgeon. You gather up your arms and
resume your pacing as calmly as before.

/MSG arriero2_llega
Another carrier, not knowing what has happened (for the first still
lies stunned), comes with the same intention of watering his mules,
and starts to take the arms off the trough.

/MSG arriero2_golpe
Without a word you raise your lance again and break his head, not in
two but in four. At the noise the whole inn comes running. The
companions of the wounded men begin to rain stones on you from a
distance, and you shelter behind your buckler.

/MSG ceremonia
The innkeeper, who wants no more trouble, decides to cut things short.
He brings the book in which he enters the straw and barley he serves
to the carriers and, with a candle end held by a boy and the two
damsels, comes to you. He bids you kneel, reads from his manual as if
he were saying some devout prayer, gives you a sound blow on the neck
and a fine slap on the back with your own sword.

One of the girls girds on your sword. You ask her name: she is called
La Tolosa, daughter of a cobbler of Toledo. You beg her to call herself
Doña Tolosa from now on. The other buckles on your spur; she is called
La Molinera, and shall be Doña Molinera. {i6}You are now a dubbed
knight!{i7}

/MSG andres_escena
Tied to an oak is a boy of about fifteen, stripped from the waist up,
and a sturdy farmer is flogging him with a belt. Each lash comes with a
rebuke and a piece of advice: {i6}"Your tongue still and your eyes
open!"{i7}

/MSG andres_libre
You set your lance at the farmer's breast: {i4}"Discourteous knight,
it ill becomes you to assail one who cannot defend himself. Untie him
at once and pay him what you owe him, if you would not have me finish
and annihilate you this very instant."{i7}

The farmer, seeing a lance over his face, unties his servant and swears
by every order of chivalry that he will pay him to the last real, and
perfumed into the bargain. Satisfied, you consider the adventure
concluded.

/MSG andres_despues
(What you don't know is that, as soon as you are out of sight, Juan
Haldudo ties Andrés to the oak again and gives him so many lashes that
he leaves him half dead. Laughing all the while. That was how you
righted wrongs.)

/MSG rocinante_elige
Following the example of the knights errant, you drop the reins and let
Rocinante choose the road. Rocinante, who knows little of chivalry but
a great deal about fodder, heads straight for his stable.

/MSG mercaderes_dulcinea
You plant Rocinante in the middle of the road and shout: {i4}"Let all
the world stand, unless all the world confess that in all the world
there is no maiden fairer than the Empress of La Mancha, the peerless
Dulcinea del Toboso!"{i7}

One of the merchants, who likes a joke, answers: {i6}"Sir knight, show
us some portrait of this lady, though it be no bigger than a grain of
wheat. And even if it shows her blind of one eye, and distilling
vermilion and sulphur from the other, we will say in her favour all
that you desire."{i7}

/MSG mercaderes_carga
{i4}"She distils nothing of the kind, vile rabble, but ambergris and
civet in cotton!"{i7} You lower your lance and charge with such fury
that, had not good fortune made Rocinante stumble and fall in the
middle of the road, it would have gone hard with the rash merchant.

You fall, and however hard you try, there is no getting up: the lance,
the buckler, the spurs and the helmet hamper you too much. A mule boy,
not too well disposed, takes your lance, breaks it to pieces and with
one of them beats you like wheat on a threshing floor.

/MSG pedro_alonso
A farmer from your own village, Pedro Alonso, comes by on his way back
from taking wheat to the mill. He finds you lying there, battered. You,
instead of complaining, recite to him the ballad of Valdovinos and the
Marquis of Mantua, and then the story of the Moor Abindarráez.

{i6}"Look, your worship, sir, sinner that I am, I am not Don Rodrigo de
Narváez nor the Marquis of Mantua, but Pedro Alonso your neighbour; and
your worship is neither Valdovinos nor Abindarráez, but the worthy
gentleman Señor Quijana."{i7}

{i4}"I know who I am,"{i7} you reply.

He sets you on his donkey and waits until nightfall to enter the
village, so that nobody will see the battered gentleman in such a poor
state of knighthood.

/MSG escrutinio1
They put you to bed and you fall asleep. While you sleep, the curate of
the village, the licentiate Pero Pérez, and the barber, Master
Nicholas, go into your library with the housekeeper and the niece to
hold a grand and diverting scrutiny of your books.

{i5}Amadis of Gaul:{i7} spared, as the best of all the books of its
kind.{n}{i5}The Exploits of Esplandian:{i7} out into the yard, for the
bonfire.{n}{i5}Palmerin of England:{i7} to be kept as a unique thing.

/MSG escrutinio2
{i5}Tirant lo Blanc:{i7} "for its style, this is the best book in the
world: here knights eat, and sleep, and die in their beds."{n}
{i5}La Galatea, by Miguel de Cervantes:{i7} "this Cervantes has been
a great friend of mine for many years, and I know he has more
experience in reverses than in verses."{n}{i5}Don Quijote de la Mancha,
Aventuras Level 10, 1989, for the Spectrum:{i7} "into the fire with
it, for nobody can finish it!"

/MSG tapiado
To cure you once and for all, the curate and the barber decide to wall
up the library, so that when you get up you will not find it; for when
the cause is removed, the effect will cease.

Two days later you get up.

/MSG freston
Your niece looks as though butter would not melt in her mouth: {i6}"There
is no library, uncle. One night an enchanter came on a cloud, got down
from a serpent he was riding, went into the room, and I don't know what
he did inside, but after a little while he flew out through the roof
and left the house full of smoke. He said he was called the sage
Muñatón."{i7}

{i4}"Freston, you mean,"{i7} you correct her. {i4}"He is a sage
enchanter, a great enemy of mine."{i7}

/MSG sancho_recluta
You promise him that, if he comes with you, before he can say knife you
will win some island and leave him governor of it. Sancho Panza, for
that is the farmer's name, leaves his wife and children and takes
service as squire to his neighbour.

{i5}"I mean to take an ass I have, a very good one, for I am not much
given to going on foot. Tonight, when the house is asleep, I'll wait
for your worship in the street with Rocinante, for I'll bring him out
through the yard door. And bring money, your worship, for nobody gets
anywhere without a penny."{i7}

And off he goes, very pleased, to tell his wife the news.

/MSG rata_muerte
Out of the cupboard comes a rat that bites you in the jugular and ends
your life. Not Amadis, not Palmerin, not Tirant: a rat. {i2}You are
dead.{i7}

/MSG rata_vive
Out of the cupboard comes a rat that tries to bite your jugular, but
the corselet stops it. The rat, now toothless, runs away. Among the
jars in the cupboard you see a key.

/MSG noche_cae
You spend the day pretending to be sane and sensible, which is the
hardest thing of all. At last night falls. The housekeeper locks up the
house and goes to bed with the key under her pillow. From the street
comes a very squire-like whistle.

/MSG sancho_calle
Sancho is waiting for you with his dapple and with Rocinante, already
saddled. {i5}"Here are some saddlebags, your worship, for the money and
the shirts."{i7} And he leaves them on the ground.

/MSG segunda_salida
You set off without taking leave of anybody. You travel so far that
night that at daybreak you are sure they will not find you even if
they search.

/MSG molinos_sancho
{i4}"Fortune is arranging matters for us better than we could have
hoped, for look there, friend Sancho Panza, where thirty or more
monstrous giants present themselves, all of whom I mean to engage in
battle and slay."{i7}

{i5}"Look, your worship, what we see there are not giants but
windmills, and what seem to be their arms are the sails."{i7}

/MSG molinos_ataque
{i4}"Fly not, cowards and vile beings, for a single knight attacks
you!"{i7} A slight breeze springs up and the great sails begin to move.
{i4}"Though ye flourish more arms than the giant Briareus, ye have to
reckon with me."{i7}

Commending yourself to your lady Dulcinea, well covered by your
buckler, lance in rest, you charge at Rocinante's fullest gallop at the
first mill. You drive your lance into the sail; the wind whirls it
round with such force that it shivers the lance to pieces, sweeping
horse and rider along with it, and you go rolling over the plain in a
sorry state.

/MSG molinos_freston
Sancho hurries to your aid as fast as his ass can go: {i5}"God bless
me! Did I not tell your worship that they were nothing but
windmills?"{i7}

{i4}"Hush, friend Sancho, the fortunes of war are more than any other
liable to frequent fluctuations. That same sage Freston who carried off
my study and books has turned these giants into mills in order to rob
me of the glory of vanquishing them."{i7}

Of your lance only the iron tip is left, lying on the ground.

/MSG lanza_nueva
You fit the iron tip of the broken lance onto the dry branch and fasten
it as best you can. You have a lance again. A little crooked, but a
lance.

/MSG frailes
Along the road come two friars of the order of Saint Benedict, mounted
on two dromedaries (for the mules they ride are no smaller), with their
travelling spectacles and their sunshades. Behind them comes a coach
with four or five men on horseback. In the coach travels a Biscayan
lady. It is perfectly clear: those black shapes are enchanters carrying
off some princess.

/MSG frailes_ataque
Without waiting for an answer, you charge the first friar so furiously
that, had he not let himself fall off his mule, he would have hit the
ground much against his will. The other spurs his castle and flies
across the plain swifter than the wind.

Sancho starts to strip the fallen friar of his habit, saying it belongs
to him lawfully as the spoils of battle. The friars' servants, who know
nothing about spoils, leave him stretched on the ground, breathless and
senseless.

/MSG vizcaino_reto
You look into the coach: {i4}"Your beauty, lady mine, may now dispose
of your person as may be most in accordance with your pleasure, for the
pride of your ravishers lies prostrate on the ground. I only ask that
you go to El Toboso and present yourself before the peerless
Dulcinea."{i7}

One of the squires of the coach, a Biscayan, seizes your lance: {i6}"Go,
caballero, ill go thou; by the God that make me, if coach thou leave
not, kill thee as here Biscayan art!"{i7}

{i4}"If thou wert a knight, as thou art none, I should have already
chastised thy folly and rashness, miserable creature."{i7}

{i6}"I no gentleman? I swear to God thou liest as I am Christian."{i7}

/MSG vizcaino_suspenso
The Biscayan takes a cushion from the coach to serve him as a shield.
You both raise your keen swords with such resolution that you seem
about to cleave the earth in two...

{i2}But the trouble of it all is that at this very point and crisis
the author of the history leaves this battle impending, excusing
himself on the ground that he could find nothing more written about
these achievements of Don Quixote.{i7}

/MSG alcana_compra
The boy sells you all the notebooks for half a real. Leafing through
them you see Arabic characters and some drawings: a knight on a hack,
long and lank and lean, and at his feet a man with a big belly, a short
body and long shanks. Underneath it says: {i6}Sancho Zancas.{i7}

/MSG alcana_traduce
The Morisco opens a notebook in the middle, reads a little and bursts
out laughing. {i6}"Here in the margin it says: This Dulcinea del
Toboso, so often mentioned in this history, had, they say, the best
hand of any woman in all La Mancha for salting pigs."{i7}

The title says: {i6}History of Don Quixote of La Mancha, written by
Cide Hamete Benengeli, an Arab historian.{i7} You promise him two
arrobas of raisins and two bushels of wheat to translate the whole of
it, and he does it in little more than a month and a half. The history
continues...

/MSG vizcaino_combate
...The first to strike is the choleric Biscayan, with such force that,
had his sword not turned in its course, that single blow would have
been enough to end your fierce combat. But good fortune makes it land
on your left shoulder: it takes away a large part of your helmet and
half your ear.

You rise in your stirrups and, gripping your sword in both hands,
bring it down on the Biscayan full on the cushion and the head, so that
he begins to bleed from his nose, his mouth and his ears, and falls
from his mule.

The ladies of the coach beg you for his life, and promise that he will
go to El Toboso to present himself before Dulcinea. Promises cost
nothing.

/MSG cabreros
The goatherds receive you kindly and offer you what they have: strips
of salted goat, roasted acorns and half a cheese harder than if it had
been made of mortar.

/MSG edad_dorada
You take a handful of acorns, gaze at them attentively and raise your
voice:

{i4}"Happy the age, happy the time, to which the ancients gave the name
of golden, not because in that fortunate age the gold so coveted in
this our iron one was gained without toil, but because they that lived
in it knew not the two words mine and thine..."{i7}

The goatherds listen to you open-mouthed and spellbound, without
understanding a word. One of them dresses your ear: he takes some
rosemary leaves, chews them, mixes them with a little salt and applies
them. It works wonders.

/MSG yangueses_llegan
Rocinante, who is not as chaste as his master, as soon as he sees a
herd of Galician ponies grazing in the meadow, trots off to make merry
with them. But the ponies receive him with hoofs and teeth, and their
owners, some carriers from Yanguas, come running with stakes and leave
him lying battered on the ground.

/MSG yangueses_paliza
{i4}"Well may you help me, friend Sancho, to take due vengeance for the
outrage that has been done to Rocinante before our very eyes."{i7}

You draw your sword and the two of you charge twenty Yanguesans. You
give the first a slash that lays open a leather jerkin. The rest come
up with their stakes and, in the twinkling of an eye, leave you both
stretched on the ground beside Rocinante, ground to powder.

/MSG venta2_llegada
Sancho carries you, slung across the dapple like a sack, to the inn,
with Rocinante tied behind. The innkeeper's wife, her daughter and an
Asturian wench called Maritornes, broad-faced, flat-headed,
snub-nosed, blind of one eye and not very sound in the other, plaster
you from top to toe.

/MSG noche_venta
At midnight Maritornes comes groping in, looking for the carrier who
is waiting for her, and finds you instead. You take her for the
daughter of the lord of the castle who, overcome by your graces, has
come to lie with you. You hold her by the wrist and explain, with great
courtesy, that your faith belongs to the peerless Dulcinea.

The carrier, jealous, deals you a terrible blow on your lantern jaws
and then trots up and down your ribs. The bed, which has little to
stand on, collapses. Maritornes hides in Sancho's bed, and he takes
her for a nightmare and starts handing out punches. The innkeeper comes
up with a lamp, and it all ends in the most comical skirmish in the
world. To crown it all, an officer of the Holy Brotherhood hits you on
the head with the lamp.

Day breaks. You have two bumps like two almonds.

/MSG balsamo_hecho
You mix the oil, the wine, the salt and the rosemary, boil them for a
good while until they seem just right, and pour it all into the flask.
You have the balsam of Fierabras. But according to your books it must
be blessed before it is taken.

/MSG balsamo_rezo
Over the flask you say more than eighty paternosters and as many
ave-marias, salves and credos, and with every word you make a cross by
way of blessing. Sancho, the innkeeper and the officer watch you very
attentively.

/MSG balsamo_beber
You drink what the pot would not hold, which will be nearly half a
quart. Hardly have you finished drinking it when you begin to vomit so
that nothing is left in your stomach, and with the pangs and spasms of
the vomiting you break into a profuse sweat. You ask them to wrap you
up and leave you alone.

You sleep for more than three hours and, when you wake, you feel
greatly relieved in body, and so much better of your bruises that you
consider yourself cured. You truly believe you have hit upon the balsam
of Fierabras. Rocinante, with rest and barley, has also recovered.

/MSG sancho_balsamo
Sancho, who takes his master's recovery for a miracle, drinks what is
left in the pot. But poor Sancho's stomach is not as delicate as
yours: before he vomits he is seized with such gripings, sweats and
faintness that he thinks his last hour has come.

{i4}"I believe, Sancho, that all this mischief comes to you from not
being dubbed a knight, for I am persuaded this liquor cannot be good
for those who are not."{i7}

{i5}"If your worship knew that, bad luck to me and all my kindred, why
did you let me taste it?"{i7}

/MSG venta2_cuenta
The innkeeper plants himself in the doorway: {i6}"Sir knight, pay me
for the straw and barley for the beasts, the supper and the beds."{i7}

{i4}"Then this is an inn? I have been deceived, for I thought it was a
castle. I cannot contravene the rule of knights errant, of whom I know
for certain that they never paid for lodging or anything else."{i7}

And without waiting for an answer you spur Rocinante and ride out of
the inn with nobody to stop you. Sancho, who has stayed behind, is not
so lucky.

/MSG manteo
As ill luck would have it, among the people at the inn there are four
wool-carders from Segovia, three needle-makers from the Colt of
Cordova and two neighbours from the Fair of Seville, lively,
well-meaning, mischievous and playful fellows. They put Sancho in the
middle of a blanket and begin to toss him up in the air and make sport
of him as they would with a dog at Shrovetide.

You see him flying above the walls of the yard, going up and coming
down with such grace and nimbleness that, if your anger allowed you,
you would laugh. When at last they let him go, Maritornes gives him a
jug of wine, paid for with her own money. The innkeeper keeps the
saddlebags in payment of what was owed him.

/MSG ejercitos
You see a great thick cloud of dust coming along the road, and from
the other side another just like it. {i4}"Seest thou that cloud of
dust, Sancho? It is all churned up by a vast army composed of various
and countless nations that comes marching there. That knight in the
yellow armour is the valiant Laurcalco, lord of the Silver Bridge; the
other, the dreaded Micocolembo, grand duke of Quirocia..."{i7}

{i5}"Well, I see nothing of the kind, sir. Maybe it's all
enchantment. But I can hear bleating."{i7}

/MSG ejercito_ataque
You set spurs to Rocinante and, lance in rest, plunge into the middle
of the squadron of sheep, and begin spearing them with as much spirit
as if you were truly spearing your mortal enemies.

The shepherds, seeing that shouting does them no good, take out their
slings and salute you with stones as big as fists. One drives two of
your ribs into your body. Another hits you in the hand and the mouth
and knocks out three or four teeth and grinders. You fall off your
horse. The shepherds gather their flock, load up the dead sheep, of
which there are more than seven, and go away.

/MSG muelas
Sancho puts his fingers into your mouth: {i5}"How many grinders used
your worship to have on this side?"{i7}

{i4}"Four, besides the back tooth, all whole and very sound."{i7}

{i5}"Well, on this side your worship has no more than two and a
half."{i7}

/MSG encamisados
Along the road comes a great number of lights, which look like nothing
so much as moving stars. They are twenty men in white shirts, all on
horseback, with lighted torches in their hands, and behind them a
litter draped in black. They murmur among themselves in low,
plaintive voices. Sancho's teeth chatter like a man with the quartan
ague.

/MSG encamisados_ataque
You plant yourself in the middle of the road, couch your lance and
charge one of the mourners. The others run off across the fields with
their lighted torches, looking like masqueraders on a carnival night.
The fallen one turns out to be the bachelor Alonso López, who is taking
a dead man to be buried in Segovia and who now has a broken leg, thanks
to you.

Sancho, who has seen it all by the light of the torches, gives you a
name: {i5}"If by chance those gentlemen should want to know who the
hero was that served them so, your worship may tell them it was the
famous Don Quixote of La Mancha, otherwise called the {i6}Knight of
the Rueful Countenance{i5}."{i7}

/MSG batanes_ruido
You want to attempt the adventure, but Sancho has slyly tied Rocinante's
legs together with his ass's halter. When you spur him, Rocinante can
only move in jumps.

{i5}"Ah, sir, heaven, moved by my tears and prayers, has ordained that
Rocinante cannot stir. Let us wait for daylight."{i7}

/MSG cuento_cabras
{i5}"There was a goatherd called Lope Ruiz, in love with a
shepherdess called Torralba... Lope Ruiz came with his three hundred
goats to cross the river Guadiana, and there was no boat but a
fisherman's little one, that held only one goat. The fisherman took
one goat across; he came back and took another; he came back again and
took another. Your worship must keep count of the goats the fisherman
is taking over, for if one slips from memory the story is at an end.
How many goats has he taken over so far?"{i7}

{i4}"How should I know?"{i7}

{i5}"Then there the story ends."{i7}

Between goats and stories, the day begins to break.

/MSG amanece
Dawn comes. Sancho, very quietly, unties Rocinante's legs, and all of a
sudden he can move again as if nothing had happened.

/MSG batanes_risa
By the light of day you discover the cause of that dreadful and
terrifying noise: six fulling hammers. Sancho holds back his laughter
with puffed-out cheeks. Then he stops holding it back.

{i5}"Thou must know, O Sancho my friend, that I was born, by Heaven's
will, in this our iron age to revive in it the age of gold..."{i7},
Sancho repeats, imitating you.

You give him two whacks with your lance that, had they fallen on his
head as they fall on his shoulders, would have freed you from paying
his wages.

/MSG yelmo_viene
Along the road comes a man on horseback wearing on his head something
that shines as if it were gold. {i4}"Dost thou not see yonder knight
coming towards us on a dappled grey steed, who has upon his head the
helmet of Mambrino?"{i7}

{i5}"What I see is a man on a grey ass like my own, with something on
his head that shines."{i7} (It is a barber who has put his basin on
his head so that the rain will not spoil his hat.)

/MSG yelmo_ataque
You charge the barber at Rocinante's full gallop, lance low:
{i4}"Defend thyself, miserable being, or yield me of thine own accord
that which is so reasonably my due!"{i7}

The barber, seeing that apparition bearing down on him, lets himself
fall off his ass and, no sooner has he touched the ground than he is up
again, nimbler than a deer, and running across the plain faster than
the wind could follow. The basin stays on the ground. Sancho, for his
part, swaps the trappings of his ass for the barber's. {i5}"Mutatio
capparum,"{i7} he says, very refined.

/MSG galeotes
Along the road come twelve men on foot, strung together like beads on
a great iron chain by the neck, and all with manacles on their hands.
They are guarded by two men on horseback with muskets and two on foot
with javelins and swords. {i5}"This is a chain of galley slaves, men
forced by the king to go to the galleys,"{i7} says Sancho.

/MSG galeotes_hablan
You ask each one the cause of his misfortune. One goes for being in
love with a basket of white linen. Another for being a musician: he
sang under torture (which is to confess). Another, an old man with a
white beard, for being a go-between and something of a sorcerer. And
the last, a very good-looking man loaded with chains, is the famous
Ginés de Pasamonte, who has written his own life in a book.

{i6}"And is the book finished?"{i7} asks Sancho.{n}{i6}"How can it be
finished, when my life is not finished yet?"{i7}

/MSG galeotes_ataque
{i4}"From all you have told me, dearest brethren, I gather clearly that
you go very much against your will."{i7} You charge the commissary so
swiftly that, before he can defend himself, you bring him to the ground
badly wounded by a lance thrust. The galley slaves break their chain
and the guards run away.

Then you gather them all together: {i4}"It is my will that, laden with
that chain, you at once set out and go to the city of El Toboso, and
there present yourselves before the lady Dulcinea..."{i7}

The answer is a hail of stones. They knock you off your horse, take a
jacket from you and Sancho's coat from him, and each goes his own way.
Sancho, fearing the Holy Brotherhood, suggests hiding in the Sierra
Morena, to the south.

/MSG rucio_robado
That night, while you sleep, Ginés de Pasamonte steals Sancho's
dapple. Or not. In the first edition the author forgot to tell it, and
Sancho went on riding the dapple for a few more pages as if nothing had
happened. The same happens here.

/MSG maleta
You open the valise. Inside there are four shirts of fine holland, a
handkerchief with a good heap of gold crowns, and the notebook, richly
bound. Sancho swoops on the crowns like a hawk. {i5}"Blessed be all
heaven, that has sent us an adventure that is good for something!"{i7}

/MSG penitencia
You imitate Amadis on the Peña Pobre. In great haste you pull off your
breeches, stand in your skin and your shirt-tail, and then, without
more ado, you cut two capers in the air and turn two somersaults, heels
over head, revealing things that make Sancho turn his head so as not
to see them again.

/MSG carta
You write in the notebook: {i6}"Sovereign and exalted lady: The
pierced by the point of absence, the wounded to the heart's core, sends
thee, sweetest Dulcinea del Toboso, the health that he himself enjoys
not... Thine till death, the Knight of the Rueful Countenance."{i7}

Underneath you add an order for Sancho's niece to give him three of
the five ass colts you left at home.

/MSG sancho_se_va
Sancho tucks the notebook into his bosom (or so he thinks), climbs onto
Rocinante and sets off for El Toboso, scattering broom branches along
the way so as to find his way back. You are left alone with your
madness.

(What neither of you knows is that the notebook has stayed with you.)

/MSG rosario
You tear a long strip from the tail of your shirt and tie eleven knots
in it, one bigger than the rest. It serves you as a rosary.

/MSG versos
You write a great many verses on the bark of the trees and in the fine
sand, all suited to your sadness. The best of them says: {i6}"Here Don
Quixote wept / for Dulcinea del / Toboso's absence."{i7} The del Toboso
part you add just in case.

/MSG princesa_llega
After three days Sancho returns. And he does not come alone: with him
are the curate and the barber of your village (with false beards made
from an ox tail that fool nobody) and a most beautiful damsel. Sancho
recites the letter from memory: {i5}"Exalted and scrubbed lady..."{i7}

The damsel kneels before you: {i6}"From this spot I will not rise, O
valiant and doughty knight, until your goodness and courtesy grant me a
boon. I am the princess Micomicona, of the great kingdom of Micomicon,
and the giant Pandafilando of the Scowl has usurped my kingdom."{i7}

/MSG princesa_acepta
{i4}"I grant and accord it, lady, and let no more be said."{i7}

You all set off together. And, as could only be expected, you end up
once again at the inn of Juan Palomeque, who turns pale at the sight of
you. The curate promises to pay for whatever damage you do. All of it.

/MSG gigante_sueno
You fall asleep and dream that you are in the kingdom of Micomicon,
before the giant Pandafilando of the Scowl. You get up in your shirt,
which does not quite cover your thighs, with a greasy red nightcap on
your head and your sword in your hand. The giant is right there in the
dark, fat, hanging from the wall...

/MSG cueros
You slash right and left. The giant bleeds! He bleeds... white. The
wineskins the innkeeper keeps hanging in the garret burst one after
another, and out of them pours a river of... skimmed milk?

{i6}"My wineskins! My red wine from Valdepeñas!"{i7} cries the
innkeeper, who has been selling skimmed milk to the carriers as good
wine for years. {i6}"I mean... my milk! I mean... my ruin!"{i7}

{i5}"He has already cut off the giant's head, I saw it myself, and the
blood ran along the floor like a river!"{i7} shouts Sancho, searching
the whole room for the head. The barber throws a bucket of cold water
over you and you wake up. The curate pays for the wineskins. And the
milk.

/MSG encantamiento
That night, while you sleep, the curate, the barber and the people of
the inn, their faces covered, tie you hand and foot and put you in a
cage of wooden bars on an ox cart. A dreadful voice, which sounds very
much like the barber's, announces:

{i6}"O Knight of the Rueful Countenance, let not the captivity in which
thou art placed afflict thee, for this must needs be, for the more
speedy accomplishment of the adventure in which thy great heart has
engaged thee..."{i7}

You accept it with great dignity: you are enchanted. Just as in the
books.

/MSG jaula_sancho
Sancho comes up to the cage: {i5}"Sir, in case your worship doesn't
know, those two masked men in front have the very same faces as the
curate and the barber of our village. And tell me one thing: has your
worship felt any need to do what no one can do for you, as the saying
goes?"{i7}

{i4}"Many times, Sancho."{i7}

{i5}"Then you are not enchanted, for enchanted people don't eat, or
sleep, or do those things!"{i7}

{i4}"There are many kinds of enchantment, Sancho."{i7}

/MSG fin_parte1
After six days you reach your village, on a Sunday at noon, with
everyone in the square. The housekeeper and the niece, seeing you thin
and sallow, stretched on a heap of hay in the cart, raise cries that
reach the heavens and curse the books of chivalry all over again.
Sancho's wife asks him whether he has brought her a petticoat; Sancho
talks to her of islands.

They put you to bed. But the annals of La Mancha say that, the third
time you left your house, you went to Saragossa...

{i6}{b1}END OF PART ONE{b0}{i7}

/MSG muerte_cansancio
So much walking on foot ends your life and you die of exhaustion. A
knight errant rides on horseback, and for good reason. {i2}You are
very dead.{i7}

/MSG muerte_ventero
{i6}"I will not tolerate your worship's insults!!"{i7}

The innkeeper pulls off a ring that has belonged to his family since
Roman times. The ring turns into a magic sword. A keen flash cuts
through the air. Seconds later, because of your insolence, your head
rolls on the floor. {i2}You are dead.{i7}

/MSG muerte_enano
Out of the foliage of the wood comes a certain dwarf, a great friend of
the farmer. He throws an axe at you and cuts off your head. {i2}You are
dead.{i7}

/MSG muerte_techo
The ceiling falls in on your head and kills you. It is a punishment
from God for insulting one of His creatures. {i2}You are dead.{i7}

/MSG muerte_carneros
A flock of rams tramples you and you die for not watching your tongue.
{i2}You are very dead.{i7}

/MSG muerte_barbero
The barber, tired of waiting, shaves your neck dry and you bleed to
death. What a silly end for so great a knight.

/MSG examinar_rocinante
A lean hack, white, with a few small black spots on his rump. He has
more cracks in his hoofs than a real has quarters. To you it seems that
neither Alexander's Bucephalus nor the Cid's Babieca can match him.

/MSG examinar_sancho
Sancho Panza: big belly, short body and long shanks. An honest man, if
that title can be given to one who is poor, but with very little salt
in his skull.

/MSG creditos
{i6}DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA{i7}{n}(c) AVENTURAS LEVEL 10{n}{n}Original
version for the ZX Spectrum (1989): Jorge Louzao Penalva.{n}Thanks to
all who helped: Angel Louzao Penalva and Luis Gabaldón Alvarez.{n}{n}
Remake 2026. Text based on the work of Miguel de Cervantes, with
liberties the author would not have approved of.
